WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.963
What actually differs between models.

2
00:00:02.243 --> 00:00:04.355
Retrieval quality is one axis.

3
00:00:04.635 --> 00:00:08.837
When you shortlist, check these too, because each has broken real systems.

4
00:00:09.117 --> 00:00:11.187
Input length and silent truncation.

5
00:00:11.467 --> 00:00:25.845
Limits range from 256 word pieces for all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (its model card says longer input is truncated by default) to 32,000 tokens for Voyage’s current models.

6
00:00:26.125 --> 00:00:31.843
A model that silently drops the second half of a long chunk can’t retrieve it by anything said there.

7
00:00:32.123 --> 00:00:35.067
Week 2’s chunking decisions depend on this number.

8
00:00:35.347 --> 00:00:37.075
Query and document roles.

9
00:00:37.355 --> 00:00:42.901
Many retrieval models are trained asymmetrically and expect you to say which input is which.

10
00:00:43.181 --> 00:00:52.269
Voyage takes input_type="query" or "document" and prepends a short instruction for each; its docs say not to omit it for retrieval.

11
00:00:52.549 --> 00:01:03.920
Gemini’s gemini-embedding-001 takes task types such as RETRIEVAL_QUERY and RETRIEVAL_DOCUMENT, and gemini-embedding-2 uses text prefixes instead.

12
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.781
Cohere has search_query and search_document.

13
00:01:07.061 --> 00:01:18.453
Open models use prefixes: E5 expects query: and passage:, Nomic expects search_query: and search_document:, and BGE adds an instruction before queries.

14
00:01:18.733 --> 00:01:22.317
Getting this wrong rarely errors; it just quietly lowers quality.

15
00:01:22.597 --> 00:01:24.389
Languages and modalities.

16
00:01:24.669 --> 00:01:33.352
If your users ask in Spanish about English documents, you need a model trained for cross-lingual retrieval, and you need Spanish queries in your test set.

17
00:01:33.632 --> 00:01:35.339
Dimensions and output types.

18
00:01:35.619 --> 00:01:37.752
Size drives storage and search cost.

19
00:01:38.032 --> 00:01:49.445
Several providers also offer int8 or binary outputs, which Voyage’s docs describe as reducing storage by 4x and 32x relative to 32-bit floats.

20
00:01:49.725 --> 00:01:51.517
Cost, latency, rate limits.

21
00:01:51.797 --> 00:01:59.925
Indexing is a one-time (or per-update) cost; query embedding sits on your request path, so its latency adds to every search.

22
00:02:00.205 --> 00:02:02.083
Data handling and lifecycle.

23
00:02:02.363 --> 00:02:08.315
Provider APIs see your text; check their data retention and training terms against your policy.

24
00:02:08.595 --> 00:02:12.328
Providers also retire models, which forces a re-embed.

25
00:02:12.608 --> 00:02:14.443
Open weights you pin don’t change.
